1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,143
Last time we saw that a continuous random variable

2
00:00:03,243 --> 00:00:06,516
gives probability zero to every single value. So how

3
00:00:06,616 --> 00:00:09,954
can we talk about its average? For discrete variables

4
00:00:10,054 --> 00:00:13,457
we summed value times probability. What replaces that?

5
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:18,036
The answer is the same idea, taken to the limit.

6
00:00:18,136 --> 00:00:21,484
And then we'll meet the simplest continuous distribution of all.

7
00:00:22,583 --> 00:00:26,501
Chop the time axis into strips. Each strip is a little bit

8
00:00:26,601 --> 00:00:30,657
like a discrete value: its probability is about f of y times

9
00:00:30,757 --> 00:00:34,814
the width of the strip. So the mean is approximately the sum

10
00:00:34,914 --> 00:00:38,209
of y times f of y times delta y, over the strips.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,302
Make the strips thinner and thinner, and the sum becomes an integral:

12
00:00:43,402 --> 00:00:46,837
the expected value is the integral of y times f of y.

13
00:00:46,937 --> 00:00:52,440
Sums become integrals, probabilities become areas, and everything else carries over.

14
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:55,673
And the picture is the one we know: the expected value

15
00:00:55,773 --> 00:00:59,274
is the point where the area under the density would balance,

16
00:00:59,374 --> 00:01:02,454
here three minutes. It sits to the right of the peak,

17
00:01:02,554 --> 00:01:05,154
pulled out by the long tail of slow payments.

18
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:11,466
Now the simplest continuous distribution. A settlement arrives at a completely random

19
00:01:11,566 --> 00:01:15,656
moment between ten o'clock and ten thirty, with no time more likely

20
00:01:15,756 --> 00:01:19,471
than any other. The density is flat. And since the total area

21
00:01:19,571 --> 00:01:23,286
must be one, its height is one over the width: one thirtieth.

22
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:28,155
Probabilities are just lengths, as fractions of the window. Between five

23
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:31,877
and fifteen minutes past: ten out of thirty, one third.

24
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:36,509
And a flat block balances right in its middle. The mean of a

25
00:01:36,609 --> 00:01:41,274
uniform is the midpoint, a plus b over two: fifteen minutes past ten.

26
00:01:42,366 --> 00:01:45,207
Its variance is b minus a squared, over twelve.

27
00:01:45,307 --> 00:01:48,775
So the standard deviation is about twenty-nine percent of

28
00:01:48,875 --> 00:01:52,467
the window's width: for a thirty-minute window, about eight

29
00:01:52,567 --> 00:01:56,660
point seven minutes. Wider window, proportionally more uncertainty.

30
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:01,617
So: for continuous variables, the mean is the integral of y

31
00:02:01,717 --> 00:02:04,844
f of y, still the balance point of the area. And

32
00:02:04,944 --> 00:02:08,677
the uniform distribution is a flat block: its mean is the

33
00:02:08,777 --> 00:02:12,644
midpoint and its variance is the width squared over twelve.

